## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for array partitioning with a per-segment objective function. It combines partition DP with range maximum queries and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over array partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation table M[a][b])
- Path reconstruction using parent pointers
- Time/space complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length-1; // 1-indexed array
  const M = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));

  // Precompute range maximums
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    M[i][i] = A[i];
    for(let j=i+1; j<=n; j++){
      M[i][j] = Math.max(M[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
    for(let i=t; i<=n; i++){
      for(let j=t-1; j<i; j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + M[j+1][i] - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let curr = n;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){
    const prev = par[curr][t];
    segments.unshift([prev+1, curr]);
    curr = prev;
  }

  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Optimization Opportunities
- Use sparse table for O(1) range max queries with O(n log n) preprocessing
- Apply divide and conquer optimization if applicable
- Consider rolling array optimization to reduce space to O(n)

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new DP angle: selecting exactly k non-overlapping substrings with a per-substring palindrome-anagram constraint, requiring fast feasibility testing via prefix masks and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Palindromable substrings via 26-bit parity mask
- Prefix XOR to test s[l..r] in O(1)
- DP over i and t with transitions from p-1
- Backpointer reconstruction; space optimization with rolling arrays
- Time complexity: O(n^2 k)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLenPalAnagram(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] ^ (1 << (s.charCodeAt(i - 1) - 97));
  function popcount(x) { let c = 0; while (x) { x &= x - 1; c++; } return c; }
  function canPal(l, r) {
    return popcount(pref[r] ^ pref[l - 1]) <= 1;
  }
  // DP skeleton: dp[i][t] = max total length through i with t substrings
}
```

